+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Codex is a terminal-based coding agent. This package includes the
OpenBSD platform patches, including V8/Code Mode, and installs the
upstream names and default ~/.codex state.

No local command sandbox is available. Managed sandbox profiles are
rejected, including profile changes during sessions. To authorize
ordinary user access explicitly, use:

    codex --sandbox danger-full-access

Commands, hooks and MCP servers then have the user's ordinary authority.
Approvals and the V8 isolate do not provide OS isolation for
subprocesses. Use a dedicated virtual machine when needed.
external-sandbox declares an external boundary; Codex does not create or
verify it.

The app-server/daemon has no `--sandbox` switch of its own. For sessions
started through it, select the same explicit mode in
~/.codex/config.toml, for example `sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access"`,
or use a declared external sandbox. Without an explicit non-managed
profile, a session that requests the default managed profile is rejected
before hooks, MCP or shell setup run.

The daemon uses the installed executable, following the official port:

    codex app-server daemon start
    codex app-server daemon version
    codex app-server daemon restart
    codex app-server daemon stop

Automatic startup defaults to disabled, as in the official port. Its
upstream flag remains available. The daemon runs as the invoking user,
inherits the starting environment and does not use an rc.d service.
State lives under CODEX_HOME; sockets use /tmp/codex-daemon-<uid>.

Update with pkg_add -u and restart the daemon. Internal updates (`codex
update`), `--from-cli` replacement and the updater loop are rejected. No
separate daemon package or private manifest is installed in
CODEX_HOME/packages.

Code Mode defaults to V8 interpreter mode on OpenBSD, preserving
JavaScript execution without runtime JIT compilation. WebAssembly was
already unavailable in the exposed runtime. Performance may differ from
JIT mode. The port no longer requests WXNEEDED: the reviewed interpreter and
builtin-loading paths use non-executable writable mappings or switch
from RW to RX. Native OpenBSD W^X and snapshot checks remain pending.
Python and Git are runtime dependencies; repository and worktree
features execute Git.
OpenBSD uses its base /bin/ksh by default, so Bash is not a runtime
dependency. Bash, Fish and Zsh completion files are generated and
installed as data. Optional npm plugins, MCP servers and hooks may
require other separately packaged executables. Plugin metadata, explicit
installation, marketplace operations and the upstream synchronisation
paths are retained. The package adds no hidden downloader or binary
installer. Review plugin and MCP sources before enabling executable
content, and do not use automatic binary installers as MCP launchers.

You may use OpenAI commercial services or misc/llama.cpp, for the last
one you should add into ~/.codex/config.toml:

	model_provider = "local"
	model = "local"
	web_search = "disabled"

	[model_providers.local]
	name = "llama-server"
	base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1"
	wire_api = "responses"

${PKGSTEM} uses a lot of memory, it starts with 256Mb per agent/worker
thread, and you may need to bump your limits (`ulimit -d`) or raise
datasize limit in used login class..

Also, ${PKGSTEM} may use a lot of open files, you may need to raise both
the system-wide maxfiles and used login class limits.

Secret Service storage needs a D-Bus session and compatible provider.
Protect CODEX_HOME and credential backups, including file-backed auth.
Browser login uses xdg-open when available; the URL is printed otherwise
and `codex login --device-auth` remains available. The generic Sh snapshot
path also covers the default OpenBSD ksh; its startup and snapshot behavior
still require native testing. Clipboard access uses X11 when DISPLAY is set.
These optional helpers are not added as runtime dependencies by this
port.

Only static review has been performed. Compilation, generated package
contents, V8, daemon, PTY, keyring and permission rejection require
actual OpenBSD validation. No claim of runtime correctness is made here.

The maintainer sandbox regression checks that managed profiles reject a
command before execution, with an explicit full-access control. Exit 77
means that OS command isolation is unsupported, even when rejection checks
pass; it is not evidence of a working sandbox.
